  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative ethnographic fieldwork within a single river basin, which may limit direct generalizability to regions with different socioecological or institutional contexts.",
      "what": "Through ethnographic research in riparian villages along the Agno River in the Philippines, this study examines how smallholders adapted their livelihoods following the construction of the San Roque Multi-Purpose Dam. It documents the specific transformations and ongoing negotiations within three key river-dependent practices: rice farming, riverine fishing, and gold panning.",
      "why": "The study introduces the concept of rhythmic livelihoods to demonstrate that a dam's long-term disruption persists not just as static material ruins, but as an active, ongoing reorganization of the temporal, spatial, and material patterns of daily survival."
